  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough Sunday...\n\nHigh pressure ridge shifts over the area today as a filling wave\nlifts into southern Idaho/the Great Basin. This will bring very\nwarm temperatures as well as increasing westerly winds aloft over\nthe region. The downslope flow will allow temperatures to climb \ninto the 80s with some locations possibly reaching or exceeding 90\ndegrees. The probability for reaching 90 degrees is greatest over\nvalley locations south and east of Billings (20% in Billings, 55%\nin Sheridan, and 30 to 50% over southern Powder River and Carter \ncounties). Record highs are possible at Livingston, Billings and \nSheridan. Along with the warm temperatures, gusty westerly winds \ncan be expected, greatest along the western foothills, where gusts\nof 25 to 35 mph are possible. The combination of the very warm \ntemperatures, gusty westerly winds and low relative humidity \n(teens), will lead to elevated fire weather concerns today. Along \nthe western foothills conditions will be critical as this area has\nseen much less precipitation over the past month, especially\nacross the lower elevations. A Red Flag Warning is in effect from\n10am to 8pm today for western areas. Use extra caution with \nanything that could start a fire. A weak dry cold front will cross\nthe area on Tuesday bringing a wind shift to the north and a cool\ndown (highs down into the 70s). \n\nHigher heights build in from the west for the rest of the work\nweek, bringing warm, dry and breezy conditions at times. Highs\nlook to range from the 70s to around 80 degrees Wednesday and\nThursday. An upper trough pushing into western Canada and the\nPacific Northwest on Friday will allow flow to amplify over the\nregion, bringing pre-frontal conditions, ahead of a potential cold\nfront Friday night/Saturday. Temperatures possibly into the 80s\nand breezy winds may bring another period of fire weather\nconcerns.\n\nHeading into the weekend, WPC cluster analysis and ensembles bring\na large upper trough into the western US, with an associated low \nmoving through the region by Sunday into early next week.\nDepending on the track of the low, much cooler conditions and\nperiods of widespread precipitation are possible (moderate to high\nchances), including mountain snowfall. Some solutions even show \nthe potential for lower elevation snowfall. The track of the low \nwill affect temperatures, precipitation amounts and type. While \nconfidence is increasing for cooler and unsettled conditions, \nthere is still plenty of time for changes to present themselves. \nThose with outdoor plans for next weekend, especially in the high \ncountry, should pay attention to the forecast through the week \nahead. STP",
